[**From what a rotation costs to which rotation is right.** The last problem does not move anything. Two permutations may each be rotated, and only the relative offset matters; every value votes for the offset that lines it up, and the answer is the largest vote.]{.flow-bridge}

### Problem: Rotation Matching {#sec-part-p5-b}

::: {.p-statement}
Two permutations $a$ and $b$ of $1..n$ ($1 \le n \le 2 \cdot 10^5$, single test case). You may cyclically shift *either* permutation left or right any number of times. Maximize the number of **matching positions** — indices $i$ with $a_i = b_i$ after the shifts — and print that maximum.

Source: [CF 1365C · Rotation Matching](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/1365/C){.solved rating="1400"}

Example: `a = [1 2 3 4 5]`, `b = [2 3 4 5 1]` → `5` (shift `b` right by 1). `a = [5 4 3 2 1]`, `b = [1 2 3 4 5]` → `1`.
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
Two shifts, one degree of freedom. Shifting $a$ by $s_1$ and $b$ by $s_2$ lines up the same pairs as keeping $a$ fixed and shifting $b$ by $k = s_2 - s_1 \pmod n$, so only $n$ relative rotations exist.

Now think per value instead of per rotation. A value $v$ sits at $\text{pos}_a(v)$ in $a$ and $\text{pos}_b(v)$ in $b$. Rotating $b$ right by $k$ moves it to $(\text{pos}_b(v) + k) \bmod n$, so $v$ matches exactly when
$$k \equiv \text{pos}_a(v) - \text{pos}_b(v) \pmod n.$$
Each value casts one vote for the rotation that matches it; the answer is the **largest vote count**. $n$ values, one histogram, $O(n)$.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **Try every rotation.** For each $k \in [0, n)$, rotate $b$ and count equal positions. $O(n^2)$, about $4 \cdot 10^{10}$ steps at $n = 2 \cdot 10^5$: far too slow, but the oracle.
2. **Offset histogram.** Build $\text{pos}_a$ and $\text{pos}_b$; for each value $v$ increment `cnt[(pos_a[v] - pos_b[v]) % n]`; print the maximum. $O(n)$.
3. **Realise the shift.** Return the winning $k$ too: rotate $b$ right by it and recount, which checks that the votes for one $k$ really all match at once. $O(n)$.

**Invariant (form 2).** After processing values $1..v$, `cnt[k]` equals the number of those values for which $k \equiv \text{pos}_a - \text{pos}_b \pmod n$. Since a value matches under rotation $k$ iff it votes for $k$, at the end `max(cnt)` is the best achievable match count. ∎
:::

The buttons are in order. Each one keeps the previous solution and changes only what that form changes.

::: {.panel-tabset .solution-forms}

