**Reading it line by line.** One detail earns its keep: the C++ residue `((x % n) + n) % n`. In C++ the sign of `%` follows the dividend, so a negative $\text{pos}_a - \text{pos}_b$ would index out of range without the lift; Python's `%` is already in $[0, n)$. The bigger idea is the histogram of a derived per-element value: when each element "wants" one parameter, count the wants instead of trying every parameter.

::: {.p-complexity}
**Try every rotation**: $O(n^2)$ time, $O(n)$ space; too slow at $n = 2 \cdot 10^5$. **Offset histogram**: $O(n)$ time, $O(n)$ extra for the two position tables and the votes. **Realise the shift**: the same $O(n)$ plus one rotation.
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- **$n = 1$**: one value votes $k = 0$; answer `1`.
- **Identical arrays**: every value votes $0$; `cnt[0] = n` — answer $n$ without moving anything.
- **Both arrays rotated arbitrarily**: shifting *both* is a no-op for the count (only $k$ matters), which is why one histogram covers all $n^2$ pairs of individual shifts.
- **Ties among offsets**: any argmax works; only the count is asked, so the histogram max is already final.
:::

