[**From rotations back to the whole chapter.** Rotation Matching reads positions through the inverse permutation, the move that also solved Split Sort; the next paragraph collects the threads.]{.flow-bridge}

**Looking back.** Every pattern in this chapter was the same two steps, *classify, then place*, with one new idea each time: a pivot and three blocks (Pattern 1), a predicate and a slot pattern (Pattern 2), a fixed prefix and a free suffix (Pattern 3), a median and an index map (Pattern 4), and blocks moved whole (Pattern 5). Two questions decided the tool every time. Does the statement require the input order inside a group? Then append into a buffer, or rotate; otherwise swap in place. And where can the arrangement fail? Usually in exactly one place, where two groups touch (the seam in Superultra, the copies of the median in Wiggle Sort II), so the proof is about that place only. The other thread was the inverse permutation, $\text{pos}[v]$: Split Sort and Rotation Matching both became counts once each value was asked where it stands, which is where @sec-idx starts.

## Pattern map

| Signal in the statement | Pattern | Section |
|---|---|---|
| "values $< x$, then $= x$, then $> x$"; two or three distinct values, any order inside a group | Dutch national flag | @sec-part-dnf, @sec-part-p1 |
| "… and keep the relative order inside each group" | read once, write cursors into a buffer | @sec-part-stable, @sec-part-p1-a |
| an operation that splits by a threshold; minimum number of such operations | count consecutive values out of order (inverse permutation) | @sec-part-p1-b |
| "alternate positive and negative", keep order | one read pass, a write cursor per class stepping by 2 | @sec-part-props, @sec-part-p2-a |
| "evens at even indices", any valid answer | two cursors on the two slot classes, swap misplaced pairs | @sec-part-p2-b |
| "next lexicographically greater arrangement" | pivot, successor, swap, reverse suffix | @sec-part-perm, @sec-part-p3-a |
| "smallest larger number with the same digits" | next permutation on the digit string, plus overflow check | @sec-part-p3-b |
| "the $k$-th permutation", "index of this permutation" | rank and unrank by factorials | @sec-part-rank |
| "$a_0 < a_1 > a_2 < \cdots$" with repeated values | median, three-way partition through the virtual index | @sec-part-virtual, @sec-part-p4-a |
| "$a_0 \le a_1 \ge a_2 \le \cdots$" | one pass of neighbour swaps | @sec-part-wiggle |
| a condition on every neighbour pair that a two-group split satisfies inside each group | partition, then guard the single seam | @sec-part-p4-b |
| "rotate by $k$ in $O(1)$ extra memory" | three reversals, juggling, or block swap | @sec-part-p5, @sec-arr-p3 |
| "stable, but $O(1)$ extra memory" | divide and conquer with rotations | @sec-part-stable-rot |
| "interleave the two halves in place" | walk the cycles of $j \mapsto (2j+1) \bmod (n+1)$ | @sec-part-shuffle |
| "sort with at most $n$ cyclic shifts of segments" | selection sort, one rotation per placement | @sec-part-p5-shiftsort |
| rotations with a price per operation | charge each carried character | @sec-part-p5-a |
| "best rotation to make two arrays agree" | each value votes for an offset; histogram | @sec-part-p5-b |

## Pitfalls

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- **Using an in-place walk when order is required.** The Dutch national flag and Hoare-style swaps get the groups right and the order inside them wrong; on LC 2161's own sample the "greater" group comes out as $14, 21, 12$ (@sec-part-p1-a). Read the statement for "relative order" before choosing.
- **Advancing `mid` after the swap with `hi`.** The value that arrived at `mid` has not been classified; skipping it leaves a wrong element in place (@sec-part-dnf).
- **Expecting Lomuto to handle equal keys.** Every element equal to the pivot goes left, so quicksort with Lomuto is quadratic on all-equal input; use Hoare or the three-way partition (@sec-part-a1-compare).
- **Strict comparisons in the next-permutation scans.** The pivot scan needs `a[i] >= a[i+1]` and the successor scan `a[j] <= a[i]`; with `<` in the second, $[1, 5, 1]$ swaps two equal $1$s and returns a *smaller* array (@sec-part-p3-a).
- **Forgetting the wrap-around**, or wrapping when the problem says not to: LC 31 wants the smallest permutation after the largest, LC 556 wants $-1$.
- **Overflow in digit problems.** The next permutation of a 10-digit number can exceed $2^{31} - 1$; parse into a 64-bit integer before comparing (@sec-part-p3-b).
- **Dealing the sorted halves in increasing order for a strict wiggle.** It fails on $[1, 2, 2, 3]$ (the two $2$s meet in the middle) and passes every odd-length test, which is why the bug survives small test suites. Deal both halves in decreasing order (@sec-part-p4-a).
- **Using $n$ instead of $n \mid 1$ as the virtual-index modulus.** For even $n$, $t \mapsto (1 + 2t) \bmod n$ visits only odd cells, twice each; the modulus must be odd (@sec-part-virtual).
- **Rotating by $k \ge n$ or by a negative $k$.** Reduce `k %= n` first (and in C++ lift negatives with `((k % n) + n) % n`); every rotation method assumes $0 \le k < n$.
- **Mixing left and right rotation.** `std::rotate(first, middle, last)` is a *left* rotation by `middle - first`; right rotation by $k$ is left rotation by $n - k$.
- **Charging a cost model per run instead of per carried element.** In Shifts and Sorting every zero pays for all the ones before it: `11000` costs $9$, not $3$ (@sec-part-p5-a).
:::

## Exercises

**1. [E] Trace the Dutch national flag on $a = [2, 1, 0, 2, 1, 0]$ with $p = 1$. Give the array and `(lo, mid, hi)` after every step.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Start `(0, 0, 5)`. `a[0] = 2 > 1`: swap with `a[5]` → $[0,1,0,2,1,2]$, `(0, 0, 4)`. `a[0] = 0 < 1`: swap with itself → `(1, 1, 4)`. `a[1] = 1`: `(1, 2, 4)`. `a[2] = 0 < 1`: swap `a[1]`, `a[2]` → $[0,0,1,2,1,2]$, `(2, 3, 4)`. `a[3] = 2 > 1`: swap with `a[4]` → $[0,0,1,1,2,2]$, `(2, 3, 3)`. `a[3] = 1`: `(2, 4, 3)`. Now `mid > hi`: done, $[0,0,1,1,2,2]$ with `lo = 2`, `mid = 4`, `hi = 3`. Six steps for six elements, as the proof says.
:::

**2. [E] Show that Lomuto's partition is not stable on $[3_a, 1, 3_b, 2]$ (pivot: the last element, $2$).**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
$j = 0$: $3_a > 2$, nothing. $j = 1$: $1 \le 2$, so $i = 0$ and swap positions $0, 1$: $[1, 3_a, 3_b, 2]$. $j = 2$: $3_b > 2$, nothing. Final swap of position $i + 1 = 1$ with the pivot: $[1, 2, 3_b, 3_a]$. The two $3$s came out in the opposite order: the final swap carried $3_a$ past $3_b$.
:::

**3. [E] Run the block-swap rotation on $[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]$ left by $k = 6$. List the rounds and check the swap count against $n - \gcd(n, k)$.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
$L = 8$, $k = 6$, $m = 2$: $k \ge m$, so swap $B = [7, 8]$ with the last two of $A$, $[5, 6]$: $[1,2,3,4,7,8,5,6]$; $5, 6$ are final; now $L = 6$, $k = 4$. $m = 2$: swap $[7, 8]$ with $[3, 4]$: $[1,2,7,8,3,4,5,6]$; $L = 4$, $k = 2$. $m = 2$: swap $[7, 8]$ with $[1, 2]$: $[7,8,1,2,3,4,5,6]$; $L = 2$, $k = 0$: stop. Swaps $2 + 2 + 2 = 6 = 8 - \gcd(8, 6)$.
:::

**4. [E] Compute the previous permutation of $[1, 3, 2]$, of $[2, 1, 3]$ and of $[1, 2, 3]$.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
$[1, 3, 2]$: the rightmost $i$ with $a_i > a_{i+1}$ is $i = 1$; the rightmost $j > 1$ with $a_j < 3$ is $j = 2$; swap: $[1, 2, 3]$; the suffix after $i$ has length one. $[2, 1, 3]$: $i = 0$, $j = 1$, swap: $[1, 2, 3]$, reverse the suffix $[2, 3] \to [3, 2]$: $[1, 3, 2]$. $[1, 2, 3]$ has no pivot, so it wraps to $[3, 2, 1]$.
:::

**5. [E] For Split Sort, find the answer for $[2, 1, 3]$, $[3, 1, 2]$ and $[3, 2, 1]$, and give one optimal sequence of thresholds for each.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Count values $v$ with $\text{pos}[v+1] < \text{pos}[v]$. $[2, 1, 3]$: pos $= (1{:}1, 2{:}0, 3{:}2)$; only $v = 1$ is backwards, answer $1$, split at $x = 2$: $[1] + [2, 3]$. $[3, 1, 2]$: pos $= (1{:}1, 2{:}2, 3{:}0)$; only $v = 2$, answer $1$, split at $x = 3$: $[1, 2] + [3]$. $[3, 2, 1]$: both $v = 1$ and $v = 2$, answer $2$, splits at $x = 2$ ($[1, 3, 2]$) and then $x = 3$ ($[1, 2, 3]$).
:::

**6. [E] For Superultra's Favorite Permutation with $n = 5$, build the residue construction and list the adjacent sums. Why must the odd block start with $5$?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Evens $2, 4$, so $E = 4$, and the head $o$ needs $4 + o \equiv 0 \pmod 3$, i.e. $o \equiv 2 \pmod 3$: among $1, 3, 5$ only $5$. The permutation is $[2, 4, 5, 1, 3]$ with sums $6, 9, 6, 4$, all composite. Head $1$ gives the seam $5$ and head $3$ gives $7$, both prime.
:::

**7. [M] Give an input where dealing the sorted halves in *increasing* order breaks a strict wiggle, and show the decreasing deal and the virtual-index partition on it.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
$[1, 2, 2, 3]$, $h = 2$: small half $[1, 2]$, large half $[2, 3]$. Increasing deal: $[1, 2, 2, 3]$, and slots $1, 2$ both hold $2$. Decreasing deal: valleys get $2, 1$, peaks get $3, 2$: $[2, 3, 1, 2]$, valid. Virtual index: median $m = 2$, modulus $5$, virtual slots are cells $1, 3, 0, 2$. The partition `[> 2 | = 2 | < 2]` over them puts $3$ in cell $1$, the two $2$s in cells $3$ and $0$, and $1$ in cell $2$: $[2, 3, 1, 2]$.
:::

**8. [M] The measured swap counts were about $n/2$ for Lomuto and about $n$ for the Dutch flag on distinct keys. Explain both numbers, and why the Dutch flag still wins on inputs with few distinct values.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Lomuto swaps once for every element $\le x$ (plus the final swap). With a random pivot, the number of such elements is uniform on $0..n-1$, so the expected count is about $n/2$. The Dutch flag swaps for every element that is not equal to $p$, in either the "less" or the "greater" branch; with distinct keys that is $n - 1$ elements. With three equally likely values only two thirds of the elements differ from $p$, so about $2n/3$ swaps. The win is not in this pass but in the recursion: all copies of the pivot form one finished block, so quicksort never touches them again, while Lomuto puts them all on one side and degrades to $\Theta(n^2)$ on all-equal input.
:::

**9. [M] For Rotation Matching with $a = [1, 3, 2, 4]$ and $b = [4, 2, 3, 1]$, compute every value's vote and the answer, and check by rotating.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
$\text{pos}_a = (1{:}0, 2{:}2, 3{:}1, 4{:}3)$, $\text{pos}_b = (1{:}3, 2{:}1, 3{:}2, 4{:}0)$. Votes $(\text{pos}_a - \text{pos}_b) \bmod 4$: value $1$ → $1$, value $2$ → $1$, value $3$ → $3$, value $4$ → $3$. The best count is $2$. Rotating $b$ right by $1$ gives $[1, 4, 2, 3]$, which agrees with $a$ at indices $0$ and $2$: exactly the values $1$ and $2$ that voted for $1$.
:::

**10. [M] Write out the three rotations that sort `11000` in Shifts and Sorting, with each window and cost. Which zero does each one carry?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
1-based windows: $[1, 3]$ turns `110` into `011`, giving `01100`, cost $3$; $[2, 4]$ gives `00110`, cost $3$; $[3, 5]$ gives `00011`, cost $3$. Total $9$. Each operation carries one zero (the last character of its window) over both ones, which is the formula's $2 + 1$ per zero.
:::

**11. [M] Change the cost model of Shifts and Sorting: every rotation costs $1$, whatever its length. What is the minimum now? Prove it.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
The number $z$ of zeros that have at least one $1$ before them. *Lower bound:* the order of a one and a zero changes only when one of them is the carried character and jumps over the other; carrying a one left over a zero only creates a new pair to undo, so each such zero must be carried at least once, and each operation carries one character. *Upper bound:* process the zeros left to right; carry each one with a single rotation from the first $1$ of the string to its own position. So the answer is $z$. The real problem's answer is the same argument with each carry priced by its window.
:::

**12. [M] Which C++ library calls give a stable two-way partition and which an unstable one? What do they cost?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
`std::partition` is unstable, linear, in place (a two-finger walk like Hoare's for bidirectional iterators). `std::stable_partition` is stable: linear time if it can allocate a buffer, otherwise $O(n\log n)$ swaps by divide and conquer with rotations (@sec-part-stable-rot). `std::stable_sort` on the predicate's value also works but costs $O(n\log n)$ comparisons. Split Sort's operation is exactly `stable_partition` with the predicate "less than $x$" (@sec-part-p1-b).
:::

**13. [M] Write out the virtual index $A(t)$ for $n = 7$ and $n = 8$, and explain why the first $\lfloor n/2 \rfloor$ values are exactly the odd cells.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
$n = 7$, modulus $7$: $1, 3, 5, 0, 2, 4, 6$. $n = 8$, modulus $9$: $1, 3, 5, 7, 0, 2, 4, 6$. For $t < \lfloor n/2 \rfloor$, $1 + 2t \le 2\lfloor n/2\rfloor - 1 < w$, so no reduction happens and the value is odd. For larger $t$, $1 + 2t$ lies between $w$ and $2w$, so $A(t) = 1 + 2t - w$, and since $w$ is odd this is even.
:::

**14. [M] Trace the stable partition by rotations on $[5, 2, 8, 3]$ with "even first". What does each level do?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Left half $[5, 2]$: halves $[5]$ (boundary $0$) and $[2]$ (boundary $2$); rotate cells $[0, 2)$ by $1$: $[2, 5]$, boundary $1$. Right half $[8, 3]$: already $[8 \mid 3]$, boundary $3$. Top level: $[2 \mid 5 \mid 8 \mid 3]$ with $F_1 = [5]$ and $T_2 = [8]$; rotate cells $[1, 3)$ by $1$: $[2, 8, 5, 3]$, boundary $2$. Evens $2, 8$ and odds $5, 3$ keep their input order.
:::

**15. [H] Prove that calling next permutation $n!$ times, starting from the sorted array of $n$ distinct values, does $O(n!)$ total work, that is $O(1)$ per call on average.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
A call's work is proportional to the length $\ell$ of the longest non-increasing suffix plus one (the two scans and the reversal stay inside it). The suffix of length $\ell$ is decreasing in exactly those permutations whose last $\ell$ values are in decreasing order: a $1/\ell!$ fraction of all permutations. So the number of calls with suffix length at least $\ell$ is at most $n!/\ell!$, and the total work is $O\left(\sum_{\ell \ge 0} n!/\ell!\right) = O(e \cdot n!)$. Hence the average is a constant, and listing all permutations costs $O(n \cdot n!)$ only because each must be printed.
:::

**16. [H] Prove that the block-swap rotation does exactly $n - \gcd(n, k)$ swaps for $0 < k < n$.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Each round with lengths $(L, k)$, $m = L - k$, swaps $\min(k, m)$ pairs and continues with $(L - k, k)$ if $k < m$, or with $(k, k - m)$ if $k \ge m$. In both cases the pair of block lengths $(k, m)$ becomes $(k, m - k)$ or $(k - m, m)$: the subtraction form of Euclid's algorithm on $(k, m)$, which keeps $\gcd(k, m) = \gcd(n, k)$ and stops when one length is $0$. Just before that, $k = m = g$, and the round swaps $g$ pairs and fixes $2g$ elements; every earlier round fixes exactly as many elements as it swaps. All $n$ elements are fixed at the end, so the swaps total $n - g$.
:::

**17. [H] Prove that a permutation of $1..n$ with every adjacent sum composite exists if and only if $n \ge 5$.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
$n = 2$: the only sum is $3$. $n = 3$: the sums are $3, 4, 5$ for the three pairs; only $\{1, 3\}$ is composite, but a path through three values needs two composite pairs. $n = 4$: the composite pair sums are $1 + 3 = 4$ and $2 + 4 = 6$ only (the others are $3, 5, 5, 7$); two disjoint pairs cannot form a path through four values. $n \ge 5$: evens first, then the odds headed by $o \in \{1, 3, 5\}$ with $E + o \equiv 0 \pmod 3$. Sums inside each block are even and at least $4$, and the seam is a multiple of $3$ that is at least $9$ (@sec-part-p4-b).
:::

**18. [H] After two in-shuffles of $6$ cards, where has the card at index $3$ gone? Answer from the cycle structure, and say after how many shuffles the deck first returns to its starting order.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
One in-shuffle sends $j$ to $(2j + 1) \bmod 7$. Index $3$ is on the cycle $3 \to 0 \to 1 \to 3$, so two shuffles take it to $1$. The other cycle is $2 \to 5 \to 4 \to 2$. Both cycles have length $3$, so after $3$ shuffles every card is home, and not before: the deck's order is the least common multiple of the cycle lengths, $\operatorname{ord}_7(2) = 3$.
:::

## Interview questions

**1. Sort an array of $0$s, $1$s and $2$s in one pass with $O(1)$ extra memory. What is the invariant, and why does the cursor `mid` not move after a swap with `hi`?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Answer"}
The Dutch national flag (@sec-part-dnf): regions $[0..\text{lo})$ all $0$, $[\text{lo}..\text{mid})$ all $1$, $[\text{mid}..\text{hi}]$ unknown, $(\text{hi}..n)$ all $2$. Each step shrinks the unknown region by one, so $n$ steps. After `swap(a[mid], a[hi])` the value at `mid` came from the unknown region and has not been classified, so `mid` must look at it again.
:::

**2. Lomuto or Hoare? Name one input where the choice matters.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Answer"}
Hoare does about a third of Lomuto's swaps and splits an all-equal array in the middle, because both fingers stop on keys equal to the pivot; Lomuto sends all equal keys to one side, so quicksort with Lomuto is $\Theta(n^2)$ on all-equal input (@sec-part-a1-compare). Lomuto is simpler and leaves the pivot at its final index, which quickselect likes. With many duplicates, use the three-way partition.
:::

**3. Can you partition stably in $O(n)$ time and $O(1)$ extra memory?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Answer"}
Not with the simple walks: Lomuto, Hoare and the Dutch flag all lose the order inside groups. The practical options are an $O(n)$ buffer (append each element to its group) or divide and conquer with rotations, $O(n\log n)$ time in place (@sec-part-stable-rot). Linear-time constant-space stable partitioning exists in the research literature but is far too intricate to write in an interview; naming the trade-off is the expected answer.
:::

**4. Rearrange an array with equal numbers of positives and negatives so that signs alternate, keeping the order inside each sign. What is the simplest $O(n)$ solution?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Answer"}
One read pass with two write cursors: a positive goes to `res[pw]`, `pw += 2`, starting at $0$; a negative to `res[nw]`, `nw += 2`, starting at $1$ (@sec-part-p2-a). Each class's slots receive its elements in input order, so it is stable. An in-place swap partition would lose that order.
:::

**5. In Sort Array by Parity II, why does one cursor running off the end mean nothing is left to fix?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Answer"}
The array has as many even values as even slots, so the number of even slots holding an odd value equals the number of odd slots holding an even value. Each swap removes one of each, so the two counts stay equal; when one cursor finds no more misplaced cells, the other has none either (@sec-part-p2-b).
:::

**6. Find the next permutation in place. Why is reversing the suffix enough, and what goes wrong with duplicates?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Answer"}
Pivot: rightmost $i$ with $a_i < a_{i+1}$; successor: rightmost $j > i$ with $a_j > a_i$; swap; reverse $a[i+1..n)$ (@sec-part-perm). The suffix is non-increasing before the swap, and the value placed at $j$ is below its left neighbour and at least its right neighbour, so it stays non-increasing and the reversal sorts it. With duplicates both scans must be non-strict; otherwise $[1, 5, 1]$ swaps two equal values and the result is smaller than the input.
:::

**7. Given an integer, find the smallest larger integer with the same digits. What are the traps?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Answer"}
Next permutation on the digit string, $O(d)$ for $d$ digits (@sec-part-p3-b). The traps: no pivot means "no answer" (no wrap-around), and the result can overflow 32 bits (parse into 64 bits, then compare with $2^{31} - 1$). Leading zeros cannot appear, because the result is larger and has as many digits.
:::

**8. How do you get the $k$-th permutation of $1..n$ without stepping $k$ times?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Answer"}
Unrank with factorials (@eq-rank): the first value is the $\lfloor k / (n-1)! \rfloor$-th smallest unused one, then continue with $k \bmod (n-1)!$. $O(n^2)$ with a list, $O(n\log n)$ with a Fenwick tree over the unused values (@sec-part-rank).
:::

**9. Wiggle Sort II: why does "sort and interleave" sometimes fail, and how do you fix it?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Answer"}
Interleaving the sorted halves in increasing order puts copies of the middle value side by side in the middle of the array: $[1, 2, 2, 3] \to [1, 2, 2, 3]$. Dealing both halves in decreasing order sends those copies to opposite ends, and they can then meet only if the value occurs more than $\lfloor n/2 \rfloor$ times, in which case no answer exists (@sec-part-p4-a). For $O(n)$ time and $O(1)$ space: quickselect the median and run a three-way partition through the virtual index $(1 + 2t) \bmod (n \mid 1)$.
:::

**10. Why is the virtual-index modulus $n \mid 1$ rather than $n$?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Answer"}
$t \mapsto 1 + 2t$ must hit every cell once, which needs an odd modulus ($\gcd(2, w) = 1$). For even $n$, the modulus $n$ would visit only odd cells, each twice. $n \mid 1$ is $n + 1$ for even $n$ and $n$ for odd $n$; with $n + 1$, the one extra residue, $n$, is never reached for $t < n$ (@sec-part-virtual).
:::

**11. Compare the three ways to rotate in $O(n)$ time and $O(1)$ space. Which do you write in an interview?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Answer"}
Three reversals: about $2n$ writes, sequential, almost impossible to get wrong; the default. Juggling: exactly $n$ writes, but jumps by $k$ and needs the $\gcd(n, k)$ cycle count. Block swap: $2(n - \gcd(n, k))$ writes in sequential runs, Euclid-shaped (@sec-part-rotate-costs). Write the reversals; mention juggling if the interviewer asks for the fewest writes.
:::

**12. A split by threshold $x$ (all values $< x$ first, both sides keeping their order) is the only operation allowed. How many are needed to sort a permutation?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Answer"}
The number of values $v$ with $\text{pos}[v+1] < \text{pos}[v]$. A split changes the relative order of $v$ and $v+1$ only when $x = v + 1$, so each backwards pair needs its own split, and splitting at each of them once sorts the array (@sec-part-p1-b). $O(n)$ with the inverse permutation.
:::

**13. You may cyclically shift either of two permutations. Why is it enough to try rotations of one of them, and how do you avoid trying all $n$?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Answer"}
Shifting $a$ by $s_1$ and $b$ by $s_2$ aligns the same pairs as shifting only $b$ by $s_2 - s_1$, so only the relative offset matters. Each value $v$ matches under exactly one offset, $(\text{pos}_a(v) - \text{pos}_b(v)) \bmod n$; count the votes and take the maximum, $O(n)$ (@sec-part-p5-b).
:::

**14. Make a stable partition with no buffer. What does rotation have to do with it?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Answer"}
Divide and conquer: partition both halves stably, giving $T_1 F_1 T_2 F_2$, then rotate the middle $F_1 T_2$ into $T_2 F_1$. Rotations move blocks without changing the order inside them, so the result is stable; the total is $O(n\log n)$ time (@sec-part-stable-rot). This is what `std::stable_partition` does when it cannot allocate memory.
:::

## Summary

::: {.callout-important title="Templates to remember"}
- **Dutch national flag:** `[< p | = p | unknown | > p]`; less → swap with `lo`, both advance; equal → `mid++`; greater → swap with `hi`, `hi--` only. $O(n)$, $O(1)$, unstable.
- **Stable partition:** read once, append to each group (or one buffer with a write cursor per group). $O(n)$ time, $O(n)$ memory.
- **Slot pattern:** a write cursor per class stepping by $2$ (stable); or two cursors on the two slot classes swapping misplaced pairs (in place).
- **Next permutation:** rightmost $a_i < a_{i+1}$; rightmost $a_j > a_i$; swap; reverse $a[i+1..)$. Previous: mirror the comparisons.
- **Rank:** $\sum c_i (n-1-i)!$ with $c_i$ the unused values below $a_i$; unrank by dividing by factorials.
- **Strict wiggle:** median $m$; three-way `[> m | = m | < m]` over cells $A(t) = (1+2t) \bmod (n \mid 1)$. Or sort and deal both halves reversed.
- **Rotation:** three reversals; juggling over $\gcd(n, k)$ cycles; block swap with $n - \gcd(n, k)$ swaps.
- **Stable in place:** partition the halves, rotate the middle block; $O(n\log n)$.
:::

- Partitioning is *classify, then place*. The classify step is a comparison or a predicate; the place step is blocks, slots, a suffix, or an index map.
- Every partition walk is proved by its invariant: name the regions before writing the cursors.
- Stability is a price, not a free property: the in-place walks lose it; a buffer or rotations buy it back (@sec-part-stable).
- "Any valid arrangement" in a statement is the licence to swap in place; "relative order" is the order to append.
- Next permutation is "change as far right as possible": the non-increasing suffix is the part that cannot change on its own.
- A two-group placement can fail only where the groups touch; find that seam (the median's copies, the even/odd boundary) and the proof is about it alone.
- Rotations move blocks without disturbing their insides, which is why they make stable rearrangement possible without a buffer.
- In-place permutations are cycles: juggling, the perfect shuffle and the virtual index all walk the cycles of an index map, the subject of @sec-idx.

